+{
+  "title": "Alex: Android XR in Three Minutes",
+  "author": "Agile Lens",
+  "source": "Original text created for the Understudy sample pack, October 28, 2026. Not a quotation or a device-readiness claim.",
+  "acts": [
+    {
+      "number": 1,
+      "roman": "I",
+      "scenes": [
+        {
+          "number": 1,
+          "roman": "I",
+          "location": "Demo walkthrough, about three minutes at 130 words per minute",
+          "entries": [
+            {
+              "kind": "stage",
+              "text": "Original rehearsal text. Pause and resume at your own pace."
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "Hi, I’m Alex. I make spatial tools for people who work with performance, film, and real spaces. I want to show you a few things we are testing on Android XR. I’ll keep the working pieces separate from the pieces that still need work.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.1"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "I’ll start with the XREAL Aura. The first thing to look for is presence: does the character feel like it is sharing this room? If the MetaHuman build is ready, I’ll use that. Watch the head, the hands, and the response when I move.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.2"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "The next part is AuraColocate. The point is a shared space. Two people should agree about where the character is. If we have a second device ready, I’ll show that agreement directly. If we don’t, I’ll tell you what we have actually tested.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.3"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "Underneath this work is our Unreal 5.8.4 fork. We have been working on foveation, live controls, and hands. I’m interested in what those changes let us do in a scene, and what the device measurements say. I’ll use the numbers from this build rather than guess.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.4"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "I also want to show the splat work from the UOXR showcase. A captured place gives us a different starting point from a modeled set. Look at the edges and what happens when I change my viewpoint. If that build is not ready, I’ll skip it and show you the next working piece.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.5"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "Stage Presence is another way into the same question: how do you feel another person in a space? I’ll show the Aura version if it has passed its device check. I’m not going to turn a build that merely compiles into a claim that it works.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.6"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "Now for a smaller display. This is the Teleprompter inside Understudy. The phone holds the controls, and the AI glasses show my next words. This is a separate glasses companion demonstration. It is not proof that the Aura runs this projected-display path.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.7"
+            },
+            {
+              "kind": "line",
+              "character": "ALEX",
+              "text": "I can pause, look at you, and pick up the thought again. I can use a short prompt or a flowing script. For an actor, teacher, or presenter, that is the test: does the tool help me stay with the person in front of me? Tell me where your attention went.",
+              "lineID": "1.1.8"
+            }
+          ]
+        }
+      ]
+    }
+  ]
+}
